Version 3.0 — A Rigorous Mathematical Foundation for Emergent World Simulation
Part I: Foundational Principles
1.1 Core Mathematical Philosophy
The simulation must achieve emergent complexity through the interaction of mathematically principled systems. We will draw from:

Stochastic processes (Markov chains, Poisson processes, Brownian motion)

Dynamical systems theory (differential equations, fixed points, bifurcations)

Game theory (utility maximization, Nash equilibria, evolutionary stable strategies)

Network theory (graph structures, diffusion, centrality)

Bayesian inference (belief updating, decision under uncertainty)

Information theory (entropy, mutual information, channel capacity)

Control theory (feedback loops, PID controllers, optimal control)

Agent-based modeling (cellular automata, Boids, swarm intelligence)

1.2 The Simulation as a Complex Adaptive System
The world is modeled as a complex adaptive system (CAS) with:

Agents: NPCs with bounded rationality

Environment: Terrain, resources, climate

Interactions: Trade, combat, social exchange

Emergence: Macro patterns from micro rules

Adaptation: Agents learn and adjust strategies

Coevolution: Agents and environment co-evolve

